.TH "TAKO" "1" "Jun 2026" "Tako CLI" "Tako CLI Manual"

.SH NAME
tako-preview-destroy - Destroy a preview and everything it owns


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtako preview destroy  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Destroy one preview: its proxy routes, containers, networks, volumes,
deployment history, service files and state, on every server it was deployed
to. Images and project-wide deploy files are shared with the base
environment and are never removed. Destroying a preview that does not exist
succeeds with status not_found.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for destroy

.PP
\fB-y\fP, \fB--yes\fP[=false]
	Approve destruction without the confirmation prompt (required in non-interactive and machine modes)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config file (default is ./tako.yaml or ./tako.json)

.PP
\fB-e\fP, \fB--env\fP=""
	environment to use (default: the only environment, else production, else the first alphabetically)

.PP
\fB--events\fP=""
	stream progress events to stdout: ndjson (human output moves to stderr)

.PP
\fB--host-key-mode\fP=""
	SSH host key verification mode: tofu, strict, ask (default: tofu)

.PP
\fB--output\fP="text"
	output format: text or json (json reserves stdout for the final result document)

.PP
\fB--preview\fP=""
	preview environment to use (deploy, ps, logs, exec, history, rollback, config explain); -e then names its base

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBtako-preview(1)\fP
